{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_og4vkxxznb575bbzkbv5h44scvqw3zduzjpzdu2fhjsy4tahksca","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification is complete and green. Summary:\n\n## Result: no divergence found — the Rust translation matches the C byte-for-byte\n\nThe C library is one translation unit (`c_src/src/driver.c`) exporting `driver(int)` and `printLine(const char*)`. Both were driven **only** through `dlopen`/`dlsym` (libloading) on the two `.so` files, so the `#[no_mangle]` wrappers are themselves under test.\n\n**Phase A artifacts** (in `translation/`):\n- `SYMBOLS.md` — `nm -D` diff C vs Rust is **empty** (`driver`, `printLine`); `ldd -r` shows no unresolved symbols. Nothing was stubbed; no C source was left untranslated.\n- `ERRORS.md` — 10 rows, derived from the only two guards in the C (`line != NULL`, `data < 100`) plus the FFI generic boundaries. No enums exist in the API (documented why).\n- `CONFIGS.md` — 23 rows covering both entry points, incl. the low-level `printLine` directly, exhaustive sweeps of `data ∈ 0..=99` and `100..=400`, format-specifier/high-bit/early-NUL payloads, and pipe/file/**unbuffered** stdout modes.\n\n**Phases B–D:** 35 tests, all passing in **all 4 configurations** (dev/release × default/`--no-default-features`; the crate declares no `[features]`, so that is the full matrix). `./run_all.sh` automates the whole matrix including the per-config symbol diff.\n\nThree things worth flagging:\n\n1. **The C's undefined behaviour is faithfully reproduced.** `driver(-1)` converts the negative `int` to a huge `size_t` for `strncpy` and dies with SIGSEGV. The Rust does the same. I test this out-of-process (`examples/runner.rs`) comparing terminating signal + stdout, and I verified that \"fixing\" the UB in Rust makes the suite **fail** — i.e. the tests enforce matching the C, not matching sanity.\n2. **Found and fixed a harness trap:** `cargo test` does *not* rebuild the `cdylib` (no test target links it), so a stale `.so` passes silently — a mutation check exposed this after an initial false \"all green\". `tests/common/mod.rs::assert_not_stale` now hard-fails on a stale artifact, and `run_all.sh` always builds first. I also had to force `RUST_TEST_THREADS=1` (via `.cargo/config.toml`) and flush libtest's own stdout, since capturing output means `dup2`-ing the process-wide fd 1.\n3. **Benign codegen difference, verified benign:** LLVM rewrites `printf(\"%s\\n\", s)` into `puts(s)`, so the Rust `.so` imports `puts` where the unoptimized C imports `printf`. Same bytes on the same stream — confirmed even with stdout switched to unbuffered mode, where the writer choice would be most visible.\n\nHarness sensitivity was proven with three reverted mutations (guard `< 100` → `<= 100`; dropped NULL check; \"fixed\" negative UB), each caught by the expected tests. `src/lib.rs` is back to its original state and needed no behavioural fix; the only crate changes were the `libloading` dev-dependency, the runner example, `.cargo/config.toml`, and the test/doc files. `c_src/` was not modified (only the `build/` directory added)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3012,"cache_read_input_tokens":99908,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3012,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"31f68d7b-76ed-425d-bd10-3f2b7531dab7","uuid":"a33ec811-fd85-4952-ac43-ff123feb1ce9","timestamp":"2026-08-28T13:27:26.238Z"}
